{
  "ID": "9.4",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "Look at the list of glycoproteins upregulated in IPMNS with both KRAS and GNAS mutations compared to wild-type IPMNs and explain the pattern with activities of signaling pathways.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-15 19:48:52",
  "elapsed_seconds": 2359.54,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "The 57 glycoproteins significantly up in KRAS + GNAS-co-mutant IPMNs cluster into two major signaling-related modules:  \n1. Mucin/O-Glycosylation machinery  \n   - Reactome ORA: “O-linked Glycosylation of Mucins” (5/65, adj p = 2.2 × 10⁻⁴) and “O-linked Glycosylation” (5/114, adj p = 1.8 × 10⁻³)  \n   - KEGG ORA: “Mucin type O-Glycan biosynthesis” (5/31, adj p = 1.6 × 10⁻⁶)  \n   - Leading enzymes: GALNT3, GALNT5, C1GALT1C1, GCNT1 and the secreted mucin MUC5AC  \n   ⇒ suggests co-mutant lesions up-regulate the glycosyltransferase network that builds the dense O-glycan coat of IPMN mucins.  \n\n2. Cell-surface receptor and adhesion signaling  \n   - Integrin interactions: Reactome “Integrin Cell Surface Interactions” (3/85, adj p = 0.03) and KEGG “Cell adhesion molecules (CAMs)” (4/142, adj p = 0.02) via ITGA6, ICAM2, BSG, MPZL1  \n   - EPH–ephrin: Reactome “EPH-ephrin Mediated Repulsion of Cells” (3/51, adj p = 0.03) and “EPH-Ephrin Signaling” (3/92, adj p = 0.03) via ADAM10, EPHA2, EPHB3  \n   - RTK/Stress modules: KEGG “Epithelial cell signaling in Helicobacter pylori” (3/68, adj p = 0.02) via ADAM10, MET; Reactome BAD/BH3 inactivation (SFN, YWHAH) and checkpoint-kinase (“Chk1/Chk2-Mediated Inactivation of Cyclin B-Cdk1,” SFN, YWHAH)  \n\nTaken together, KRAS + GNAS-mutant IPMNs do not simply express more mucins; they reprogram the glycosylation apparatus (GALNTs/C1GALT1C1/GCNT1) and co-upregulate cell-surface receptors (integrins, EPH/RTKs) whose functions are heavily modulated by complex O-glycans. This dual reinforcement of a barrier-forming mucin coat and of glycan-tuned receptor/adhesion signaling likely cooperates to drive ductal epithelial remodeling, altered polarity and early neoplastic progression.",
